Day 17 — Correction sheet
Topic	Your answer	Correction
throw vs throws	throw handles an error through an exception	throw raises an exception. catch handles it. throws declares possible propagation.
Generic invariance	List<Number> might accept ArrayList<Integer> because Number is the parent	It does not compile. List<Integer> is not a subtype of List<Number>.
Generic method vs class	Class T sets class type; method T sets parameter type	A method's <T> is its own type parameter, usable for parameters, return values, and local declarations.
Why counting zeroes is insufficient	The last element might be non-zero and need moving	Counting gives the number of zeroes, but doesn't preserve and reposition the non-zero elements.
In-place requirement	“Return the array with zeroes at the end”	The method returns void. It modifies the existing array.
One Java nuance worth remembering
Your finally explanation from Day 16 was mostly correct. finally normally runs when control leaves try or catch, including when an exception propagates or a return occurs. But it isn't guaranteed if the JVM terminates abruptly.